  {
    "id": "/how-to-trade/nvda#0017",
    "url": "https://www.thetradehq.com/how-to-trade/nvda",
    "kind": "paragraph",
    "sourceKey": "sections.3.p.0",
    "text": "Imagine a $10,000 worksheet with $2,000 held directly in NVDA and $8,000 in a fund. Assume, only for this exercise, that the fund has a 10% NVDA weight. The indirect exposure is $800, so combined NVDA exposure is $2,800, or 28% of total value. Counting two account rows misses this overlap. A selected 10% NVDA fall contributes $280 of loss through the two exposures using the initial exposures and assuming other holdings stay unchanged. Real fund weights need a dated holdings source; the assumed 10% is not a current portfolio fact.",
    "sha256": "449dcfb858439c512a575455b4b6b2325661e3e9c37d7da1dd1a9cce37537281",
